WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.163 --> 00:00:03.010
(tribal drumming)

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.473
- What is a soundsuit?

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.780
A soundsuit is this sort
of wearable sculpture.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:12.290
It can be very still,

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.610
and yet it can be extremely animated.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.690
It's a suit of armor,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.723
a retaliation vestment,

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.670
a political statement.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:25.503
It's a shaman.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:33.120
(quiet orchestral music)

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.770
The first soundsuit
came out of frustration

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:41.770
in my sort of concern as
a black male in America

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:47.130
feeling devalued, discarded, dismissed.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.650
I was sitting in the park one day,

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:53.360
and I'm just emotionally struggling

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.710
with the Rodney King incident.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:58.810
And I happened to look down at the ground,

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:02.600
and there was this twig;
and I looked at that twig

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.440
and I thought it was something discarded.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.440
That symbolically represented
how I felt as a black male.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:17.830
This simple twig was the
catalyst for my first soundsuit.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.550
And so then I proceeded to collect

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:21.720
all the twigs in the park,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.230
took them back to the studio,

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.320
started to build this sculpture,

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.120
and the moment that I put it on the body,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.230
it started to move.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.970
It then made sound,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.970
which then led me to this other expression

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.270
that was focused around protest.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.523
In order to be heard,
you gotta speak louder.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:47.083
When I made the first soundsuit,

00:01:48.604 --> 00:01:51.600
I knew by just looking at it

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.283
that it would change my life.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.363
I could tell that this
was something other.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.260
And my first exhibition was in '96,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.220
and it was 30 soundsuits;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.640
and they basically sold that
entire show within a month.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:19.360
This was like this overnight
sensation, I swear to God.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:27.310
Today, I'm able to work in this vast arena

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.510
working between bronze and wood

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.723
and fiber and performance.

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.800
In the studio at any given time,

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.670
there's always eight assistants

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.843
just prepping and getting materials ready.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.903
I'm very particular about
how things are built.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.300
It's deliberate; every
move is critically decided.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.300
It's a sort of collage of
elements coming together.

00:03:02.273 --> 00:03:05.577
♪ Walking home ♪

00:03:05.577 --> 00:03:08.654
♪ Walking home ♪

00:03:08.654 --> 00:03:10.650
♪ Walking home ♪

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.770
We are in the Park
Armory in New York City.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.120
We are in the beginning phases

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.313
of a performance titled Upright.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.870
Upright is this performance

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.760
that really is this
sort of rite of passage

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.230
that is accompanied by 12 dancers

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:35.853
and a gospel choir from Harlem.

00:03:36.838 --> 00:03:40.300
(choir singing and clapping)

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.970
The part of the process of collaboration

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:47.103
is the most gratifying thing that I do.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.420
I like to bring a project to a city

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:53.763
and hire the city to build the project.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:59.620
There's a lot of work that
is gathered from narratives

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.570
and stories within the communities.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:07.570
All of this is all folded
into a larger sort of project.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.223
So that's why I do it.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.280
My consciousness being awakened

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.280
has allowed me to sort of find purpose.

00:04:19.427 --> 00:04:22.760
(slow, emotional music)

